ices it's very easy to lose money  in that particular trading activity if  you get things wrong so cho has done  particularly well on that front as well  now this is have inevitably led to some  calls for a full tax on oil and gas  companies as did bps strong results  earlier this week now there's something  of a row being going on in cabinet over  this the chancellor rishi sunak has made  clear that he would like to see oil and  gas companies recycling more of those  profits into the uk if they are to see  off the threat of a win full tax now bp  did that earlier this week they  announced that between now and the end  of the decade it will be investing some  18 billion pounds here in the uk now  shell has not gone into details today  about how much it will be investing in  the uk but in total it's expecting to  invest between 20 and 25 billion pounds  in the country between now and the end  of the decade much of that according to  ben van burden the chief executive will  be in renewables products  energy companies are in the spotlight  this week but the private sector more  broadly has a huge role to play in  helping the world hit its climate  targets now just a reminder these are  the big numbers that we're playing with  now and to slow climate change we need  to stop this number the temperature from  going up and to do that we need to make  sure that carbon emissions  cut or at least balanced out by 2050.  now esg that's environmental social and  governance is well it's a big part of  what businesses do the world over it's  what they do beyond purely seeking  profit and climate action is a big part  of that and one organization esg book  has collated emissions data from the  hundred biggest companies in each region  of the world and it's come up with some  surprising numbers um so let's just take  a look at this chart now and you can see  the companies in the asia pacific region  and the middle east have uh made  virtually no dent in their emissions at  all in the past seven years north  america and europe have done well a  little better but still way off the  targets they need uh to be aiming for  but interestingly companies in  sub-saharan africa and even more so  latin america well they're leading the  way and joining me now is to explain a  little bit more is todd bridges global  head of esg researcher albert wesk who  helped collate the data uh tom  thank you very much for joining us and  it's really interesting isn't it that  the line about the sub-saharan countries  and uh latin america are leading the way  could you explain to us why that's  happening  yeah perfect and it's a pleasure to chat  with you um yeah this research is based  on a series of multiple years of data  acquisition by esg book on scope one and  two data  for hundreds of companies across region  and what we have found is that over a  time period as you just introduced  2015 to 2020  the scope 1 2 data  node  suggests that companies by region are  reacting differently  and in particular  south africa  as a nation we have a number of data  points within that region and companies  in particular  that are showing progress right we're  looking at over time about a 20  reduction on average  for those companies within the sample  um same thing in latin tam we have  approximately 35 to 40 reduction again  with the companies reported  it's very important to  use these research and these studies to  make potential conclusions but for us  you know it's mission critical that we  as a as a global community of investors  uh and corporations speak directly to  each other about actual disclosed data  and so it's it's important this type of  research comes to question  you know which companies are reporting  which companies are not reporting and  can we make these conclusions about the  suggestion data findings and todd what  do you think needs to be done or  improved for those companies that aren't  reaching their their climate goals or  targets  well i think probably as you know and  the viewers of this audience know that  there are  significant strides being made across  policy regulators  exchanges  nation states  and in the companies themselves across  those regions  what we have been trying to help  facilitate through the design and build  a vsg book as a technology platform  is direct disclosure on these  climate scope data for example or more  broadly esg data  it's important for companies to  communicate and disclose at scale  you know again  there are leaders in the field who are  putting out this high quality data into  public markets through either formal  filings or through their websites  but what we have designed and built for  the last two and a half three years is a  technology disclosure platform to help  facilitate that disclosure across  companies not just public companies but  also privates  so again data data disclosure is  paramount as we try to  make this transition of the economy from  a brown economy to a green economy if  you will  and the companies need to be able to  have the tools at their disposal  to signal to the markets what they're  doing and  quantitatively speaking you know at the  data level okay todd thank you very much  for your input today  thank you  well let's get some of the day's other  climate news now because baghdad has  been hit by a severe sandstorm stopping  flights from the airport and sending  dozens of people to hospital with  breathing problems now shops were closed  on the streets of iraq's capital as the  storm restricted visibility and anyone  venturing out onto the streets wore a  mask drought and declining rainfall  means sand storms are becoming more  frequent in iraq with several hitting  the country over the past month  a global alliance has been launched in  support of vanuatu's bid for the  international courts of justice to  consider the human rights impact of  climate change more than 1 500  organizations from 130 countries are  backing the pacific island nation's  action vanuatu itself at high risk of  climate impact says the icj needs to  protect vulnerable communities and the  rights of residents who are at the front  lines of climate change  millions of birds have disappeared over  the past two decades due to the  destruction of their habitats climate  change and pollution a review led by  manchester metropolitan university found  that of around 11 000 bird species  populations of half of them are falling  and one exception is among water birds  where populations living in wetlands in  europe and north america have risen 13  since 1917.  and flying insects have declined nearly  60 in less than 20 years in the uk the  kent wildlife trust and bug life use the  windscreen phenomenon to find out what's  happening to bugs in the uk the survey  used data from drivers who reported the  quantity of squashed insects they found  on their car number plates after driving  a certain  distance  well the climate crisis is ever more  urgent and the people coming up with the  solutions are getting younger it seems  in the annual raspberry pi competition  children as young as eight were asked to  design tech that helped save the planet  [Music]  hi we are the raspberry pioneers we are  team raspberry reef hi we are sunflower  solar our invention is to help  electricity from solar panels get around  the world by  creating the time zone so the solar  panel moves in the direction of the sun  and we've created a domesticated house  plant where people were gardening during  provide and lock down however now that  everything's come back to normal there's  a lot less and a lot less produce is  being made and the prices are inflating  at really high rates and therefore we  decided that if we create something to  help people grow their own produce we  can control this let's be inspired by  their willingness to experiment their  willingness to have a go their  willingness to have impact we've  invented a capsule which helps protect  and sustain coral reefs we're looking  for different environmental changes  which can damage coral reefs and the  main two are ph and temperature so we've  invented the capture which can measure  temperature and when it detects the  temperature has changed by too much for  too long it will release calcium  carbonate solution which is what corals  use to grow their exoskeletons who are  we  to question  what is clearly the most important issue  on the agenda of children today we need  to give them the space to innovate and  the space to have impact  well do stay with us coming up  i'll be joined by founder of terror  praxis kirsty gogan and chidi oti  obihara who's a senior finance  researcher at project draw down we'll be  discussing what shells should be doing  with those bumper profits and which  countries are leading the way in  corporate climate action  [Music]  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news so let's get  straight into some of those climate  issues with founder of terror practice  kirsty gogan and chidi oti obihara a  senior finance researcher at project  draw down thank you so much for joining  us today  pleasure to be here thanks for having us  let's kick off with the the first big  story so the announcement of bumper  profits from shell this morning so  similar to bp earlier in the week is  increasing the pressure on the  government to implement a win for tax um  kirsty i'll start with you  what do you think  shell should do with their profits where  should they put that money  well i think you know the government  does have um a real need to address the  very near-term crisis that people are  experiencing with the cost of living and  particularly the you know incredible  increases we've seen in energy costs  that are pushing millions of people into  energy poverty in this country right now  and you know the opportunity for a  windfall tax on the recent profits the  record-breaking profits  does seem like a sensible  option that should be considered now at  the same time the energy companies do  also have you know a real need to invest  in the sort of longer term  crisis that's also unfolding uh which is  the climate crisis and the need to  invest in the clean energy transition so  there may be a way to combine these two  um these two requirements by  earmarking some of that uh windfall  uh for investment in clean energy and  energy efficiency that can help protect  consumers in future from the kind of  price volatility and crunch that we've  seen recently as well as supporting um  the the most vulnerable people in the  near term as well  chidi there will be lots of people  watching tonight and they'll be thinking  you know what they could do with a bit  of help with their energy bills do you  think a windfall tax would help those  people  well there's a pallet of things that  government can do um government can  either uh impose a windfall tax on  companies like shell  which uh the italians this morning for  example they impose 25 win full tax on  their energy companies uh the french  also uh did something similar they  placed a cap on edf uh worth about 8.5  billion euros  so you can either impose windfall tax on  the companies or you can cap the amount  of money they charge consumers either  way you you buffer consumers from this  really large exogenous shock um i think  policymakers have to make a choice by  doing one or the other but not doing  either is is going to be really  challenging uh we spoke on this program  a couple months ago when i talked about  how it's really obvious that there are  new routes out of energy poverty that  don't involve renewables and here we are  you know months later uh living that  reality because while we listen to large  corporate titans like shell  talk about you know declaring nine  billion dollars in in in profits and  talk about how they intend to put uh  something like five or six percent of  that uh uh towards transition we know  that bp for example is putting 12  of that towards transition we know that  total energy is putting 20  of its free cash flows towards  transition we need to get to the point  we start to have these detailed  conversations  about what proportion of profits that  these companies use to actually affect  transition  and we need to get to the point where we  say  it's time to stop investing  in new fossil fuel projects and only  exclusively invest in new renewable  projects because the cost dynamics make  it really clear this is the right  business decision to make there's a  really interesting uh report which i'm  sure that kirsty knows about that lazard  wrote a couple years ago and they've  updated last year it's the levelized  cost electricity report and in it um you  know uh large-scale solar energy  is you know possibly the cheapest energy  that's available right now it's sort of  20 something dollars for the  whereas what shell is doing what other  entities are doing are you so much more  expensive it's actually both wasteful  and painful to keep investing in new uh  fossil fuels what shell will be saying  kirsty is that you know they're willing  to invest we said it early didn't we  that they're willing to invest from 20  to 25 billion in the uk in the next  decade in low carbon energy  ministers will of course have to keep a  close eye on whether that happens they  say it's a good deal what do you say  i think it's you know it's really  important that these companies are  investing but i do think that there is  an important role for government and you  know  organizations like ours in defining you  know where the investment should be  going we've put a lot of emphasis on  investment in the electricity sector  decarbonizing the electricity sector and  wind and solar as chidi said have been  very successful in becoming quite cheap  and you know being delivered at scale  which is fantastic  but the reality is is that the  electricity sector is only 20 of our  energy use and actually you k